From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] rtc: apple-pmu: allow a firmware-driven suspend

The driver refuses every sleep state but s2idle, because nothing routed
the alarm as a wake source in any other. A firmware-driven suspend does
that itself: on T8140 the alarm is wake source NUB.SPMI0.SW3, and
macOS's RTC wakes come back through it with the same alarm registers
(measured under aura).

The suspend path clears the driver's refusal only when the platform has
said it is driving the suspend, so other machines keep refusing.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>

[j604 backport: resolved: this tree's driver still uses the baku_* names (Aurora renamed them apple_pmu_*); same logic; Aurora's tools/asahi/pmu-rtc-tests.py is not in this tree and is dropped]
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5

---
 drivers/rtc/rtc-apple-pmu.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/rtc/rtc-apple-pmu.c b/drivers/rtc/rtc-apple-pmu.c
index 2e3161a914..f821edf4d8 100644
--- a/drivers/rtc/rtc-apple-pmu.c
+++ b/drivers/rtc/rtc-apple-pmu.c
@@ -280,7 +280,14 @@ static int baku_rtc_prepare(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct baku_rtc *baku = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
-	if (pm_suspend_target_state != PM_SUSPEND_TO_IDLE || baku->io_failed)
+	if (baku->io_failed)
+		return -EBUSY;
+	/*
+	 * A firmware-driven suspend programs the wake sources itself, and the
+	 * alarm stays armed in the PMU, which stays powered. Only a sleep state
+	 * without either has no way to bring the alarm back.
+	 */
+	if (pm_suspend_target_state != PM_SUSPEND_TO_IDLE && !pm_suspend_via_firmware())
 		return -EBUSY;
 	return 0;
 }
